==Original Issue==

If you are looking at a very long article, like looking at the Napoleonic Wars, and it has so many headings. I think we need to implement some sort of outline at the top	. As a first iteration, let's make it so that there is, if there is more than 3 major headings, major meaning top-level headings, then all of the top-level headings are listed in an outline at the top of the article and they link to jump to that heading within the app. This outline does not need any play buttons. The intent is that you can tap on that. It takes you to the text. You can just press the play button that is there. There are three or fewer major headings, Then there's nothing to do. View looks exactly the same as it does now. I think when it's that short, we don't need to add another UI thing at the top

==Status Report: 2026-10-10 23:31 PDT==
IMPLEMENTED.

A tappable "Contents" outline now renders at the top of the article, but only
when the article has more than three top-level headings — where "top-level" means
h2 sections (`ArticleSection.level == 2 && heading != null`, the lead section is
level 1 and is excluded). With three or fewer, the view is unchanged, exactly as
requested.

How it works:
- `ArticleContent` computes `topLevelHeadings` from the sections and shows a
  compact `ContentsOutline` (a "Contents" header followed by each top-level
  heading as a tap-to-jump row) above the `LazyColumn`, inside a new wrapping
  `Column`. No play buttons on the outline by design — tapping scrolls straight
  to that heading (via `animateScrollToItem(index, scrollOffset = 0)`), where the
  existing per-heading play button lives.
- Crucially, the outline is rendered *above* the `LazyColumn`, not as `LazyColumn`
  items, so `indexFor(...)` and the follow-mode / "jump to current" scrolling from
  ticket 06 remain byte-for-byte unchanged. No offset bookkeeping, no regressions
  to the existing index tests.

Verified: `./gradlew assembleDebug` compiles and `./gradlew testDebugUnitTest`
passes (all existing tests, including `IndexForTest` and `SubtreeOfTest`, still
green). Note: the existing "parameter 'title' is never used" warning on `indexFor`
pre-dates this change and is harmless.

Things to review on-device:
- The outline sits above the scrollable list, so it stays visible at the top
  while you scroll the article below it. If you'd prefer it to scroll *with* the
  article (as an in-list lead item), that's a small follow-up — it just requires
  offsetting `indexFor`, which I deliberately avoided to keep follow-mode stable.

